        "date_published": "2026-10-03T16:00:00+00:00",
        "description": "<p class=\"is-style-drop-cap-with-border\">Is there life elsewhere, or is Earth unique in the universe? This is one of humanity\u2019s great unanswered questions.</p>\n<p>One way to approach this mystery is to investigate how life began here on Earth. Our research explores the conditions where a mixture of molecules somehow transformed from chemistry into biology.</p>\n<p>In <a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-026-70700-5\">a new study</a> published in <em>Scientific Reports</em>, we shed new light on one possible step in this process: how a fragile sugar from outer space may have helped create the conditions for its own survival on Earth, where it eventually became the backbone of one of life\u2019s most important molecules.</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://nautil.us/how-sugar-from-outer-space-became-one-of-the-backbones-of-life-on-earth-1285504/\">How Sugar from Outer Space Became One of the Backbones of Life on\u00a0Earth</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://nautil.us\">Nautilus</a>.</p>",
